WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980
Hey, what's up guys?

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:12.900
Before we start this week's episode, I just wanted to start, before we go into the game, I wanted to talk about Dante had wrote into us, a real person, not the guy from Devil May Cry.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:23.140
Sick name, bro. Dante wrote in to contact at NostalgiaCast.com, just like you can, and let us know that he beat Gumshoe on a legit NES.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.660
If you remember, that was a challenge I issued.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.640
I issued that challenge on the Gumshoe episode.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.480
He said, hey guys, love the show.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:40.820
I am currently only listening to the episodes for games I've played, but I like the show enough that I'm thinking about getting caught up with some more older games so I can listen to those episodes too.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.100
I like this guy.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.740
He's pretty smart. Do it.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.700
And anyway, I just caught the Gumshoe episode, and Mike said he didn't believe anyone could beat Gumshoe legitimately.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.400
I happen to have a retro gaming blog where I talk about only those games I've beaten.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.680
And I'm willing to plug this blog in the show notes just because I think it's a really cool blog because here's the thing.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:08.600
Dante's a collector, specifically of NES games, but the whole justification behind buying the NES games is he has to beat them.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:21.060
If he's going to spend the money on collecting these old NES games, he wants to beat them, and so therefore this blog is just essentially like him showing the internet that he's beating these games.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:31.660
And it's really cool to see these actual photos on a legit NES on a CRT of him beating Gumshoe and even goes on to further rub it into my face.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:39.620
I haven't listened to your Ghosts and Goblins episode yet, but just in case you challenged the listeners to beat that one twice to get the true ending, see?

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.100
Dante knows. Here's my proof for that as well.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.520
Did we challenge?

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.440
I don't think I challenged.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.300
I just said for the three people that are listening to the podcast that have beaten.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.020
We did do a lot of grumbling.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.940
Yeah. So yeah, just that's really cool that Dante wrote into us.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.340
Thanks so much for writing in.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.420
And guys, write in more often because you'll be featured in the very front of the episode.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.120
For everybody else who wanted to skip, I'm sure you noticed the other chapter markers by now, so just skip around wherever you like.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:15.560
I think it'd be really funny if you went between me saying, and now it's time for the essential games list and the beginning where I say, and welcome to NEStalgia.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.400
Because I'm about to say that in 30 seconds.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.520
Thanks. Bye. I'm going to write in.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.180
Put on your black belt.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.740
Sharpen your split second reflexes.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.000
Focus your concentration, karate champ.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:49.740
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:50.800
I'm Mike.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.200
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.140
What is that about, Sean?

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.960
You're always doing, and I'm Sean.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.160
I said, I'm Sean.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.760
You're not want me. Yeah, that time you didn't.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.020
Oh, shoot. Now I just sound like I'm really aggressive.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.940
I'm just thinking of it again. What in God?

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.300
You'll have to excuse me. I've been fighting people in a video game called Karate Champ for a while now, so I'm just very aggressive in general.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.500
It's been a weird time since we started playing Karate Champ, hasn't it?

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.800
You know, the world is different now because I've played Karate Champ, a Data East game.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.080
Did you play Karate Champ?

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.060
Well, here's the thing.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.400
About Karate Champ. Yeah, you play Karate Champ if you can.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.420
You know, like, I made a reference to this last week when we were talking about an excellent,

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.460
essential game called Gradius.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.780
And the thing that was really cool about that is that's a game where, like, you push buttons

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:41.460
and things happen.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.120
Like in a rational, like, sequence way.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:46.740
Yeah, exactly.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.620
You know, I'm cracking up.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.560
For the record, guys, we don't rehearse these episodes.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.960
I hope it doesn't come off that way either.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.280
Absolutely does not.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.980
Right. But we don't rehearse these episodes.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.020
So sometimes when I'm hearing these guys' thoughts, I realize what they're saying for, like, the very first time.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.840
And, like, Joe saying in the right direction.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:04.860
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:33.800
We're explaining that. We're explaining that.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.320
You know, the only thing that you make a successful strike is given a point.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.400
And then you have to get...

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.500
It stops and resets. Yeah, stops and resets.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.520
Which is like a real karate strike.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.140
Well, yeah, in that way, it's a sports simulator.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.280
Exactly. Yeah, in that way, Sean.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.460
Don't get too excited. In that way.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.020
Yeah. No, I'm just saying, you're ready to say it's a sports game and you're getting excited.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.860
No, I'm not. You know, so that means that there are no health bar.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:54.980
There's no hit points.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.600
The only thing you need to do is successfully land a hit.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:06.320
And then you, once you get, like, enough points, you beat the player and then you go to another person who looks exactly the same.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.760
And then you go to another person who looks exactly the same.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.700
But the environments change up quite nicely.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.160
There's quite a bit of...

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.580
They're cute, aren't they?

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.080
...of design in these environments.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.380
I think I liked the palm trees and the orange sunset.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.640
My favorite background is the volcano.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.220
Yeah, well, screw the volcano.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.560
The volcano, I don't know.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.500
Yeah, there's a...

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:28.900
Well, like, here's the thing.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.120
The volcano's over to the right.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.660
It's, like, just kind of, like, out...

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.020
Like, I don't know if it's a window or if this is just like...

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.980
No, you're kind of like you're in front of a building and then there's a volcano in the background.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.400
Okay, maybe it's not a volcano.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.360
Maybe it's Mount Everest.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.820
But the idea...

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:41.780
Mount Everest. Yeah.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.040
Okay. It was a volcano to me and, of course, that's a throwback to Gradius because Joe hated the volcano.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.580
I actually was going to make a joke about that before I realized that you were making a joke about that.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.280
Yeah, yeah. Well, making a joke or just being an idiot, you decide.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.340
I definitely thought it was a volcano until Sean gave me, like, a...

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.280
Don't do that.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.060
Like, it's not a volcano.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.720
Sean, your favorite background.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.160
It was, uh...

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:15.220
In the first place that you fight, you are sort of on this cliff outcropping that makes it really uncomfortable.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.640
Like, it looks like you can fall off at any moment.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.640
But, of course, that's not a feature.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:24.580
And it's probably, uh, for the best that you can't fall off.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.120
But it looks cool.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.240
It's fine. This game is terrible, but it looks fine.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.360
I probably would have jumped off if it was an action.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.840
Yeah, I would have jumped off the cliff.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.720
All right, now that we know our favorite backgrounds, I think we can just jump right into the essential games list.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.580
Uh, I probably even played the music there for fun.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.800
But no, we're not doing that yet.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.160
Uh, let's get first impressions if we haven't, like, led you on enough already.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.480
Uh, Joe, besides your favorite background, how else did you feel about this game?

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:55.460
Um, so, my first match, I tried to change up my moves a little bit.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.320
I didn't really do anything.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.400
And sometimes, like, a lot of the time, if I tried to change up the move, I would just kick...

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.300
I would do a back kick, even though my opponent was in front of me.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.360
I couldn't control which direction I was kicking, which is why...

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.680
You must have hit the B button.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.620
Yes. The B button only kicks backwards.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.460
It only attacks backwards, if you can believe it.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.520
But then, like, the A button sometimes punches, sometimes kicks.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.040
I guess it depends on how close you are.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.840
Well, it's also, you know, a directional input.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:23.580
Is it with the direction?

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.640
Yeah, they're combo moves, in a way.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.000
I don't even think I realized that.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.300
Well, you're... The thing is, though, that that is a...

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.640
It's a system that just doesn't work.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.400
Right. That's what I was gonna say.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.540
Yeah. It's like, yeah, it's supposed to be a directional thing, but it doesn't always guarantee.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.480
For me, it was almost exclusively kicks, which would have been great if I even understood

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.340
how I was making contact...

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:44.740
...against the other player.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:45.820
Well, sometimes you just don't.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.860
Yeah, that was my other initial thing, was that it, um...

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.000
I couldn't tell what was determining if I hit them or not.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:59.520
Apparently, in the manual, uh, if you don't hold the button down long enough, you will just fail your move.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:00.680
And it'll...

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.640
it won't count as anything.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.580
Or it'll count as a block on their behalf.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.640
Um, but...

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.580
All that aside, um, this game...

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.360
Like, whether you learn the rules and learn the moves or not, it...

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.320
it doesn't matter, because it...

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.880
Like, there's no hit detection.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.760
Um, I feel like our...

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:30.920
Like, me and the opponent's sprites would just be overlapping for several seconds, and then we'd be facing in opposite directions, and hitting and flailing, and nothing would happen.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:35.420
Um, so whether you know what you're supposed to do or not, that's not what's going to happen.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.900
But did you notice that when you were facing in opposite directions, the opponent was able to turn and hit you?

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.120
Yeah. I mean, I couldn't figure out how to do that.

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.840
Uh, that's a round kick, Joe.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.640
No, but I'm... no, I'm saying, actually, he would be facing me, but I would be still in my back turn facing the way I was at the beginning.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:51.780
Oh, and he would...

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:53.920
He would be facing me and just be doing regular punches and kicks.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.020
Well, Joe, in karate, it's disrespectful to turn and face your opponent.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:02.480
Everyone's back to back. But I think it's also disrespectful to hit your opponent in the back of the head.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:11.140
Like, and, uh, I also noticed at one point I just left the menu screen on too long, and you go to the little demo, and it plays, you know, the demo plays for you.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:19.100
And the guy in the white gi in the demo also couldn't figure out how to kick in the right direction.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:21.280
Like, several times he was just kicking back.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.520
Like, even the guy demoing the game didn't know how to play the game.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.420
You think that's... wait, hold on.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:30.640
Wait. Do you think that, like, there's a guy that, like, recorded his own play?

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.080
The guy on screen. There's a guy on screen.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.740
Okay. He's the demo guy.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:34.980
Yeah. And he's kicking...

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.120
Oh, okay. Okay. He's kicking in the wrong direction.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.820
The guy who's trying to show you this is what the game could be like shows you exactly what the game could be like.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.600
Yeah, exactly. Your character while the computer's controlling it.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.020
Um, Sean, do you have any different first impressions?

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.880
I think I turned that into a combination first impression there.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:58.200
Okay. Uh, my first impressions was cause I, like, should I, should I look at a manual?

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:09.840
Like, like, I really just wasn't even sure if I was playing a game or if, like, I was just part of, like, one of those, like, experiences where you're just learning that, like, you know, in life it's better not to fight.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:17.640
Like, it's better to just be the calm one, stay still, like, let your opponent who's bullying you take the match.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.600
They win the match, but you win in life, you know?

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:25.360
That's right. So I went to the manual, and, uh, there I found out that there's, like, a billion moves.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:29.620
Yeah. Like, there's so many different moves because it's, like, which way you move your joystick.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.460
If you don't move your joystick at all and you just hit, like, A.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.620
If you don't move your joystick and you hit B.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:36.800
Or if you hit A and B at the same time.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.380
Something that, by the way, we haven't seen yet.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:39.460
So that's kind of cool.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:43.640
Yeah. Like, the idea of hitting two buttons at once to get a move to happen.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.340
In theory. Uh, if it ever worked.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.620
Yeah. I, I really thought I was, like, dyslexic.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:53.180
Because I'm looking at the manual and I'm trying to do these inputs.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.900
And still, at best, I'm doing kicks that turn me the other way.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:00.160
I got A, B was roundhouse kick, right?

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.260
I think that was really the only one.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.080
Yeah, A, B was round kick.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:08.100
Yeah. I think some of them have to do with, like, the proximity to the player, to your opponent.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:10.760
So sometimes. The manual doesn't mention that.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:11.920
Really? Just throwing that out there.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.300
I'm not saying you're wrong. I'm just saying, like, for me.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.240
I guess I'm, maybe I'm just prescribing logic where none exists.

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:23.940
Yeah. Well, I mean, sometimes, like, A, B, though, can do, like, A plus B can do other things,

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.480
too. Like, you know, like a low punch or something like that.

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.160
If you're, like, squatting down.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:33.020
Yeah. You know, like, you can kind of do, like, a below the belt punch, which is weird.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.340
I don't think that's allowed in karate either.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:39.960
And the second time that I, like, the first time I lost, second time I started over and

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:45.220
I played, and I just, every single round would take, like, the exact amount of steps forward,

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.060
one punch, and, like, most of the time that would hit them.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:51.260
Like, there was no reason for me to do anything else but that.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.640
And, like, it would hit, like, a little more than half the time, so I won.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:58.660
It's sad, though, that, like, you know, and they're two different games.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.780
This is, like, a fighting game in a weird way, like how Urban Champion is kind of a fighting

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:10.240
game. But, like, Kung Fu, like, that gets, that has just almost as many options of, like, what

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:11.080
you can do, right?

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:16.360
Right. With, like, the high kick, the sweeping kick, the punch and the punch down below, like the

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:17.460
squat punch. Right. Jump kick.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:19.140
Yeah, the jump kick, all that stuff.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.360
Like, all the different moves. And for the most part, they detect okay.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:23.440
Yeah. And it feels great, too.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:24.860
You know, and it feels good when you're doing it.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.480
Right, right, right. And it's fast. It's interesting.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.220
Here, it's like, you know, I always just felt like I was one step behind the enemy, even

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:33.720
from the very beginning of the game.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:39.620
Like, I just feel like my hits just, either the hit boxes are awful or the hit detection

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:45.560
is awful. Like, one of those two things must be true because you can't suck at a game from the very

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:50.180
beginning. You know, like, you can't, like, you know, the first time you boot up a game and, like,

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:55.000
just try it out. You can't, you can't just be so awful that, like, it's the equivalent of, like, getting

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:59.780
to Super Mario 64 and the first thing you do, right, you get into the courtyard, you

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:00.800
don't even enter the castle yet.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.220
Just drown. You just go to the water.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.040
You drown yourself because Mario doesn't sink.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.760
You just go down to the water and drown yourself.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.860
You know, like, that would be, like, the equivalent for what this is in Karate Champ, but it happens.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:19.140
Yeah. Um, I think that, uh, it has a, let me just, let's turn it around a little bit.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.560
Sure. Turn it around for me.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:22.180
I have one happy thought about this game.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.720
This game looks alright.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:25.940
We talked about the backgrounds.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:26.940
Okay, that's my happy thought.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:33.880
It's got some really good, uh, as far as, um, we've seen so far on the NES, some, some

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:35.460
good, like, voice clips.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:36.800
Oh, that was cool. Yeah.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:37.840
I actually didn't mind that.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:38.780
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I do like that.

